dupa 2, response: NOWhile performing tick checks is an important part of preventing tick-borne diseases, the claim is medically inaccurate for a couple of reasons. First, tick checks do not *prevent* tick bites; rather, they help identify and remove ticks that have already bitten/attached to the skin, which helps prevent the transmission of diseases like Lyme disease. To actually prevent bites, one should use insect repellents (like DEET or picaridin) and wear protective clothing. Second, standard medical advice (such as from the CDC) generally recommends conducting a full-body tick check after coming indoors and at least once a day, rather than strictly every 2-3 hours.
